The fix is complete. Both `go vet` and `go test ./llm/...` pass.

## Summary

**Root cause:** In `llm/llama_server.go`, `memoryParsingWriter.updateRunnerMemoryLocked()` simply summed *every* buffer size reported by `llama-server`. When a model is memory-mapped and only partially offloaded, the weights get reported twice: once as the `CPU_Mapped`/`<DEV>_Mapped` buffer (the page-cache mapping of the on-disk file) and again as the device model buffer (the offloaded copy of those same weights). `ollama ps` therefore showed roughly 2× the real weight memory.

**Fix:** Split model-weight buffers into three groups:
- `*_Mapped` (file-backed page-cache mappings)
- `*_REPACK` (real repacked copies — kept in full)
- other plain model buffers (e.g. the offloaded device model buffer)

When a `*_Mapped` buffer is present and the model file size is known, the `mapped + plain-model` group is clamped to the on-disk file size so the weights count **once**. KV/compute/output buffers and `_REPACK` copies are always kept in full. VRAM (`gpu`/`vramByDevice`) is unchanged — only the inflated *total* was corrected.

This matches all six test scenarios (partial offload with mmap on CUDA & Metal, CPU-only mmap+repack, no-mmap, missing model file, and mapped buffers that don't overlap), without touching any `_test.go` files.
